1 Introduction

1.1 Theoretical difficulties of the standard model

The ΛCDM model is highly compatible with a large body of cosmological observations, yet its theoretical architecture relies on several independent exogenous assumptions:

Initial singularity: General relativity diverges there and the theory breaks down. A physical theory that contains an indescribable mathematical singularity at its core suggests that the theory is incomplete.

Cosmic inflation: It introduces a scalar field without a known physical counterpart, whose potential form must be manually adjusted to match observational data.

Dark matter: Unknown particles introduced to explain galactic dynamics and gravitational lensing, which have not been directly detected in any experiment to date.

Dark energy: An unknown energy density introduced to explain the accelerated expansion of the universe, whose physical nature is completely unknown.

These four components are not derived from a deeper principle but are added layer by layer to fit observations. This situation suggests that spacetime itself may not be a fundamental entity, but rather a macroscopic emergent phenomenon of some deeper discrete structure.

1.2 Basic picture of TDMU

The Topological Dynamic Membrane Universe model (TDMU) proposes an alternative path: the universe is modelled as a topological interface (membrane) emerging from a discrete combinatorial network. The network consists of nodes and directed edges, with the only dynamics being local reconnections. Within this framework:

space is the sum of distance relations among structures on the membrane;

time is the cumulative count of reconnection events;

matter consists of stable loop‑woven subgraphs (topological knots);

gravity is the large‑number statistical effect of the total number of shared links;

light is a dynamical transverse wave perturbation propagating in the shared‑link network;

the root of accelerated expansion is the topological tension coupling between the membrane and the external non‑woven substrate, rather than any intrinsic energy density.

The stability of the membrane is maintained by a topological gradient transition zone (GTZ) of finite thickness, within which the loop density continuously transitions from the internal woven state to the external non‑woven state. The evolution of the characteristic thickness 
